<section class="reference-section" id="ajamila"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Ajamila · The Power of Narayana’s Name</h2><a href="/ajamila.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 6 · chapters 1–3.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi <em>Srimad Bhagavat Mahapuran</em>, Volume 1. The private source PDF is not copied into the public website.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://archive.org/details/srimad-bhagavat-mahapuran-2-volume-set-sanskrit-hindi/Srimad%20Bhagavat%20Mahapuran%20Volume%201%20Sanskrit%20Hindi/mode/2up" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Read the supplied first volume</span><small>Internet Archive · Gita Press · Sanskrit with Hindi explanation</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>6.1.20–39 · private PDF pp. 1103–1106</strong><small>Ajamila’s old age, his youngest son Narayana, the arrival of Yama’s messengers, his cry and the intervention of Vishnu’s messengers.</small></li><li><strong>6.1.40–68 · private PDF pp. 1106–1110</strong><small>The first debate about dharma and consequence, followed by the account of Ajamila’s disciplined youth and later wrongdoing.</small></li><li><strong>6.2.1–19 · private PDF pp. 1112–1115</strong><small>The Vishnudutas on the divine name, atonement and transformation at the level of the heart.</small></li><li><strong>6.2.20–38 · private PDF pp. 1115–1118</strong><small>Ajamila is freed, hears the exchange, remembers the people he harmed, repents and resolves to redirect his life.</small></li><li><strong>6.2.39–49 · private PDF pp. 1118–1119</strong><small>His journey to Gangadvara, disciplined contemplation, later vision of the Vishnudutas and the narrative conclusion.</small></li><li><strong>6.3.1–18 · private PDF pp. 1126–1129</strong><small>The Yamadutas question Yama; Yama locates his own authority within the order of Narayana.</small></li><li><strong>6.3.19–35 · private PDF pp. 1129–1133</strong><small>Yama’s instruction concerning devotion, the divine name and whom his messengers should not approach.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> The interrupted death, debate, repentance, Gangadvara practice and later liberation belong to the source narrative. The reflection about using mercy responsibly is editorial. The page avoids treating Ajamila’s partner as the cause of his conduct and keeps responsibility with Ajamila’s choices.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. Faces, architecture and landscape are imagined. The cords, conch, discus and lotus use symbolic rather than literal visual language.</p><a href="/mahapuranas.html#bhagavata">Meet the Bhagavata Purana</a></div></section>
